WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.876 --> 00:00:02.825
- [Man] You take, you know, probably
the worst turbulence

00:00:02.825 --> 00:00:05.843
you've kind of experienced
and multiple it by about 10,

00:00:05.843 --> 00:00:07.613
and that's the potential
that we're going into.

00:00:07.613 --> 00:00:11.030
(light orchestral music)

00:00:14.741 --> 00:00:16.721
- [Narrator] That's the
voice of Justin Kibbey.

00:00:16.721 --> 00:00:18.196
He's a pilot.

00:00:18.196 --> 00:00:21.958
- [Justin] My job title
says Hurricane Hunter pilot.

00:00:21.958 --> 00:00:25.261
- [Narrator] Yeah, he flies
planes through hurricanes.

00:00:25.261 --> 00:00:27.599
It's all in the name of science.

00:00:27.599 --> 00:00:29.013
He works for NOAA.

00:00:29.013 --> 00:00:30.554
- [Justin] The National Oceanic

00:00:30.554 --> 00:00:32.496
and Atmospheric Administration.

00:00:32.496 --> 00:00:34.323
- [Narrator] These missions
are for data gathering,

00:00:34.323 --> 00:00:37.304
to track and study hurricane
paths and movements.

00:00:37.304 --> 00:00:40.752
But Justin Kibbey and I
aren't talking about data.

00:00:40.752 --> 00:00:42.693
We're talking about what it feels like

00:00:42.693 --> 00:00:45.943
to fly an airplane through a hurricane.

00:00:47.158 --> 00:00:50.003
(ominous orchestral music)

00:00:50.003 --> 00:00:51.305
- [Justin] It starts to get cloudy.

00:00:51.305 --> 00:00:53.905
And once you're in the storm,

00:00:53.905 --> 00:00:57.564
you'll start to hear rain
hitting the windscreen.

00:00:57.564 --> 00:00:59.231
It can be very loud.

00:01:00.427 --> 00:01:04.394
(rain falling and thunder rumbling)

00:01:04.394 --> 00:01:07.233
It surrounds you, it
surrounds the whole plane.

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:08.361
Probably the best way to describe it

00:01:08.361 --> 00:01:12.830
is if you have ever lived
in a house with a tin roof.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:16.303
It's hard to hold a conversation inside.

00:01:16.303 --> 00:01:17.249
- [Narrator] These are the conditions

00:01:17.249 --> 00:01:19.132
Hurricane Hunters fly through

00:01:19.132 --> 00:01:21.259
every time they go on a mission,

00:01:21.259 --> 00:01:23.831
because the point of
flying through a hurricane

00:01:23.831 --> 00:01:27.164
is to fly through it and get to the eye.

00:01:29.321 --> 00:01:33.352
And he was telling me about the
first time he ever did that.

00:01:33.352 --> 00:01:36.073
- [Justin] And that was 2010.

00:01:36.073 --> 00:01:38.023
We were flying into Hurricane Earl.

00:01:38.023 --> 00:01:39.339
- [Man] What's the latest information

00:01:39.339 --> 00:01:40.992
we're getting about Earl, Chad?

00:01:40.992 --> 00:01:45.765
- [Chad] 135 miles per hour
and still getting stronger.

00:01:45.765 --> 00:01:46.871
- [Justin] And then, I
remember breaking out

00:01:46.871 --> 00:01:49.576
into the eye of the storm.

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:51.934
Everything just kinda stopped.

00:01:51.934 --> 00:01:53.905
Everything. The noise stopped, the lightning stopped.

00:01:53.905 --> 00:01:57.226
It was pitch black out, you
could see stars above you.

00:01:57.226 --> 00:01:59.479
It was really beautiful,
serene; it was quiet,

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:03.646
(soft piano music)
and it was a holy shit moment.

00:02:04.846 --> 00:02:05.788
What did we just do?

00:02:05.788 --> 00:02:07.610
What just happened?

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:11.777
And a bolt of lightning lit up
the entire outbound eye wall

00:02:13.104 --> 00:02:15.805
where I could see it visually.

00:02:15.805 --> 00:02:16.967
And all of a sudden, I was like,

00:02:16.967 --> 00:02:21.134
"Oh man, that's gonna
be scary." (laughing)

00:02:22.171 --> 00:02:23.528
It was a rough ride on the way out,

00:02:23.528 --> 00:02:25.476
but I'll never forget it.

00:02:25.476 --> 00:02:29.643
It was probably one of the
coolest that I've been on.

